// SPDX-License-Identifier: LGPL-3.0-or-later
//
// A `u8?` field storing 255 reads back as null — and that is the RULE, not a collision.
//
// This file was an `@EXPECT_FAIL` for years, filed as "@PLN25 deferred (LOW): the extreme
// doubles as the in-band null sentinel", on the reading that a `u8?` ought to hold 255 and the
// packed field was failing to.  `formal/types.md` says the opposite and always did — the narrow
// sentinel is "excluded from `τ?`'s non-null range" — so the field was the only position
// implementing the rule, and the item's premise inverted it.  The local, the element, the
// argument and the return all kept 255 as a number, so an ordinary in-range `250 + 5` was
// destroyed on the way into a field with nothing to say so.  The defect was the DISAGREEMENT,
// and it pointed the other way (loft#1249, @FR-N-Reserve).
//
// It stays as a guard rather than being deleted because the shape it pins — the sentinel
// arriving through a struct LITERAL — is the one that made it look like a storage bug.
// `tests/scripts/1249-a-nullable-narrow-sentinel-is-not-a-value.loft` is the full sweep.
//
// @falsified-at: none — the assertions read the same on every build back to the one that filed
//   this file; what changed is which answer counts as correct, so no earlier build can fail it.
//   Its control is the non-nullable cell below, which fails on any build that reserves an edge
//   it should not.
struct Holder {
  x: u8?,
  y: u8,
}

fn main() {
  h = Holder { x: 255, y: 255 };
  assert(h.x == null, "255 is the null of a u8? field, got {h.x}");
  assert((h.x ?? 7) == 7, "and `??` recovers from it, got {h.x ?? 7}");
  assert(h.y == 255, "…while a NON-null u8 field holds 255, which reserves nothing");

  g = Holder { x: 254, y: 254 };
  assert(g.x == 254, "254 is an ordinary u8? value, got {g.x}");
  assert(g.y == 254, "and an ordinary u8 value, got {g.y}");
}
